WEBVTT

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.150
<i>It's been over a month now.</i>

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.990
<i>Richard still hasn't returned.</i>

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.720
<i>You trust people too much.</i>

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.590
<i>Sometimes it hurts you, 
but that's how life is.</i>

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.270
<i>Remember that.</i>

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.340
Why did he say that?

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.110
Huh?

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.640
The notice is gone.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.030
Richard?

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.830
Welcome.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.010
<u>Episode 9</u>
<b>The Alexandrite's Secret</b>

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.460
The store is still preparing 
to open. I'm very sorry.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.040
I'm not a customer.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.390
I'm Nakata. I used to work here part-time.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.440
Ah, you. I've heard about you.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.320
You must be Richard's mentor, Ranashinha-san.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.930
He called me his mentor?

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.740
Yes.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.910
Indeed, Mr. Detective.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.870
My name is Saul Ranashinha Ali.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.960
Nakata Seigi.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.000
Um, where's Richard? He's back, right?

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.460
No. He asked me to look after the store.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.840
I don't currently have any 
plans to hire a part-timer.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.180
I'm sorry.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.480
Where is Richard? What is he doing?

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.480
I don't know anything.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.630
Did he leave without saying anything to you?

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.060
We spoke briefly over the phone.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.320
What did he say?

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.320
I'm sorry. I'd prefer not to say.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.690
This will probably be a long conversation.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.330
Why don't we discuss things over tea?

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:55.470
Nakata-san,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.210
why do you want to know where he is?

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.050
Why?

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:07.090
I can't imagine there's much point 
in learning his current situation.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.690
I just want to know how Richard is doing.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.390
I want to understand things.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:21.990
Wow, that's strong...

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.200
It's Sri Lankan-style spiced tea.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.410
He liked royal milk tea, didn't he?

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.800
Yes. He taught me how to make it.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.130
Did he also happen to tell you
 about the origin of this shop's name?

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.000
No.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.400
"Étranger" is French for "foreigner."

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.010
When I asked him why he chose such a name,

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:50.440
he said it was because he felt like 
a foreigner no matter where he went.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.980
Pompous, don't you think?

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.690
Foreigners are lonely people.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.520
From the beginning, 
this store was for him alone.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.160
I can't imagine that's why Richard left.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.720
Everyone has their reasons.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.130
You say you want to understand,

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.900
but no matter how unreasonable 
or incomprehensible,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.540
there are things we have 
to accept. That's life.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.360
I understand what you're saying, but—

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.860
Nakata-san, try asking yourself this question.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.510
Why do you want to see Richard?

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.670
Do you want an apology?

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.830
Or are you looking to 
restore your relationship?

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.760
You look awfully gloomy.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:44.230
Huh? Oh...

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.840
Tanimoto-san.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.670
I can lend an ear if you'd like.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.580
Richard...

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.840
Someone important to me—I'm not sure
 what I'd call him—suddenly disappeared.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.460
I don't know what to do.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.280
I want to go after him,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.840
but I'm not sure what I hope 
to accomplish by doing that.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:18.960
I have no idea why I'm such a mess.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.760
Really? I have a feeling I know.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:24.480
Huh?

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.240
You're angry, Seigi-kun.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.280
I am?

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.390
You feel betrayed by someone 
you love, and it frustrates you.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.800
You don't understand why 
they didn't tell you anything.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.360
But you think it's wrong 
to be angry with someone

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.000
when you don't know their circumstances.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.530
You're heartbroken at the thought 
that you might not have been

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.980
as important to them as they were to you,

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.430
but you're suppressing that feeling.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:00.590
Because your heart is broken, 
you don't know what's real anymore.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.520
Did I get that right?

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:12.270
He... He has no idea how worried I am!

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.830
If he weren't such a good guy...

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.800
I wanted to be able to 
help him out someday, too.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.430
He must be to you like you are to me.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.000
If you suddenly disappeared,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.690
I wouldn't accept it. I'd go after you.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.060
After all, there's no way 
you wouldn't be hurting.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.030
I'm going to look for Richard.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.090
I might not find him...

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.730
and if I do, he might not be happy to see me.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.820
Yeah?

00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:59.900
Thank you, Tanimoto-san.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.340
I really like you.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.380
I hope you find Richard-san.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.590
You're going after Richard?

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.500
Yes. I've steeled my resolve.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.820
Did you come to an answer after 
our conversation the other day?

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.560
What will you do when you see him?

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:23.890
I'm going to tell him how mad I am.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.940
Well, aren't you just overflowing 
with youthful energy?

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.050
Have some red bean jelly and cool your head.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.320
It goes well with spiced tea.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.760
I know Richard's family is 
arguing over the inheritance.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.200
I want to be with him so I can support him.

00:08:40.910 --> 00:08:43.240
So you can support him?

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:48.890
The Claremont family owns land in London's

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.230
preeminent financial district.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:55.380
They're a distinguished family that earns 
a tremendous amount off of real estate.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.940
Their job is to protect 
their status and wealth

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.650
and pass it down to the next generation.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.850
He lives in a completely 
different world than you.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.550
I know that, but—

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.020
You <i>don't</i> know.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:14.660
Do you really think there's anything a 
college student like you can do for him?

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.510
Know your limits.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.470
There is something I can do.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.120
And what is that?

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.250
I can make pudding.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:30.080
He likes sweet things when he's tired.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.050
Honestly...

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:35.440
This is why I hate cleaning up after 
that idiot apprentice of mine.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.420
He asked me to protect you,

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.020
but if you're determined to leave,
 then I'm not responsible for you.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:47.580
He asked you to protect me? Richard did?

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.300
His situation is just that complicated.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:57.300
I met him four years ago 
in Ratnapura, Sri Lanka.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.720
Where his grandmother's from...

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.450
My first impression of him was terrible.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:08.460
He was using a fake name 
to sell gemstones to tourists.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.650
Was it Edward Baxter?

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:16.140
He said being called Richard 
brought back unpleasant memories.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.890
His methods were insane.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:23.150
He'd sell expensive gemstones on the cheap,

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:28.270
but sell cheap gemstones at exorbitant 
prices to people he didn't like.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.930
Why did he do that?

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.200
Who knows?

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:37.280
But it seemed to me that he was in despair.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:42.280
Granted, he had a keen eye for gemstones.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:48.390
His brilliance struck a chord with me, 
and he was fluent in multiple languages.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:52.460
So I proposed that he become my apprentice.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.330
Otherwise, I told him to return to England,

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:01.340
and he firmly insisted on anything but that.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.040
It's that bad?

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.020
What about his family?

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:08.060
Estranged for the past four years.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:11.640
He doesn't even like using 
the Claremont family name.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:17.360
But then he tells me he's heading home...

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.450
Richard's in London?

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:25.940
Not even I know exactly where in London.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.450
That's plenty.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:31.460
I'm going to find Richard.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.710
And if he needs my help...

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.000
Make him pudding or whatever else he wants.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:39.260
I will.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.420
But be on your guard.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:46.620
There are already people 
watching you carefully.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.100
The Claremont family?

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.260
Good for you.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.850
Come back safe, Nakata-san.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.360
Help me see Richard again, Grandma.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.870
Is this your first time in the lounge?

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:29.390
Yes, it is...

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.680
Good evening.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.960
Your Japanese is really good!

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.020
Thank you.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:42.490
I'm not sure exactly why, but they told me 
I was being upgraded to business class.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:46.040
How very lucky for you, Nakata Seigi-kun.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:46.740
Huh?

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.080
I arranged for the upgrade 
on your connecting flight.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.580
After all, it's a long flight to London.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.630
Please, don't glare at me.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.360
I'm Richard's cousin, Jeffrey.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:04.100
Although I feel more like his older brother.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:04.930
Nice to meet you.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:11.930
You want to know why Richard 
disappeared, don't you?

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.720
For the first time in four years,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.650
he transferred some money out 
of his English bank account.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.440
In Japanese currency, 
it was about five million yen.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.610
Five million...

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.820
<i>I'm pleased that I was able to help.</i>

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.850
My father contacted him through the bank.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:35.740
My father was Richard's foster parent. 
He owes him a great deal.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.550
He probably couldn't refuse him.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:38.830
Foster parent?

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:42.860
Richard's parents divorced 
when he was young.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:47.030
He lived with me and my older brother 
Henry like we were one big family.

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:47.840
Look...

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.990
This is Richard.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.970
He was cool and smart as a kid.

00:13:54.380 --> 00:14:00.500
But when his dog Taro died, he cried 
his eyes out into his pillow in his room.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:02.340
Too cute, right?

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:05.900
Does this mean you followed me?

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:13.620
Before I tell you why I'm here, 
I have a few things to share.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.040
Richard's told me about the 
dispute over the inheritance.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.750
Well, a lot has happened.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.210
Did you know our grandmother 
is from Sri Lanka?

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:27.920
Yes.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:32.120
When our grandfather married
 Grandmother Leah,

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.660
the furious count disowned him.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.140
He was stripped of his right to the title.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:37.850
What?

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:42.920
What I'm about to tell you is my family's story

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:45.440
of gemstones and hatred.

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:49.540
My father is the ninth generation 
of the Claremont family.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.330
Richard's father was the third son.
An entomologist.

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:58.350
His mother was a blindingly 
gorgeous French actress.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:01.620
Our grandfather was the eighth generation,

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.580
and our great-grandfather the seventh.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:06.300
But as I just mentioned,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.340
there was discord between our 
grandfather and great-grandfather.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:12.670
Our great-grandfather had only one son,

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:17.190
so he planned to pass his title
on to someone else's child.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:18.140
However...

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:24.620
The World War began, and the London 
bombings nearly wiped out our family.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:28.800
Our great-grandfather was left badly 
injured and on the brink of death.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:33.670
If a count dies without an heir,

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.320
his title and land are forfeited to the state.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:42.460
In order to prevent that, relatives 
summoned our disowned grandfather

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:45.170
to England from Sri Lanka.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:49.200
Much to his disappointment, he inherited 
the title from the late seventh count...

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.970
and became the eighth himself.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.680
Would you like to sleep? Or may I continue?

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:58.970
Keep going.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:04.320
Eight years ago, 
Richard was a college student.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.360
With the death of the family's trustee,

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:12.520
our great-grandfather's secret will 
and fortune were unsealed.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:17.310
Inside the safe was a 118-carat diamond.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.370
Its market price was 300 million pounds.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:25.200
At current rates, that would be 
about 40 billion Japanese yen.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:26.880
Forty billion?!

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:31.590
In England, the eldest child 
inherits the entire fortune

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.640
to prevent it from being divided.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.070
Then the diamond...

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:40.140
Normally, my father would have inherited it,

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:44.460
but there was an extremely odd 
condition written into the will.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:45.270
What?

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:48.610
"At the time of the will's unsealing 
during the next count's generation,

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:53.770
whoever's mother's birthplace is closest 
to England is to inherit the diamond."

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.610
Their mother's birthplace?

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:57.370
Yes.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:00.280
The farther from England 
our mothers were born,

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.940
the less of a claim we have
 to the inheritance.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.690
Richard's mother was French.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.570
Ours was American.

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:11.270
America is farther away than France.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:12.210
In other words...

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.130
Richard gets the diamond.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.550
Bingo!

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:17.920
But one more thing.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.050
In order to inherit the diamond,

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:24.730
he must marry a traditional 
English housewife.

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.980
What? What kind of condition is that?

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.860
He'll inherit the diamond once married,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:34.950
and there's a detailed list concerning
 valid birthplaces for his spouse.

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.910
According to our family lawyer,

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:41.950
virtually no one but a traditional English 
housewife would be acceptable.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:46.050
What if Richard wants to marry 
someone who doesn't fit the bill?

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.700
Then no one will inherit the diamond.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:51.210
It will be donated to an 
environmentalist group.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:54.500
The same goes if he refuses to inherit it.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.700
Goodbye, 300 million pounds!

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.220
The inheritance can't be carried over.

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:01.140
That's awful.

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:02.800
Isn't it?

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:08.360
The entire family should inherit 
the 300-million-pound diamond.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:12.500
He can solve everything by 
marrying an appropriate person

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:16.010
and returning the diamond to the family.

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.990
You want him to marry 
someone he doesn't even love?

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.920
This isn't the nineteenth century.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.400
Once he inherits the diamond,

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:27.660
he can divorce her and run off 
with whomever he likes.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:31.460
That's all he needs to do, 
but he's so stubborn.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.670
I finally get it.

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.120
That's why Richard made that clause.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:40.940
Answer me this one thing.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:43.120
How far has your relationship with him gone?

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:44.220
Huh?

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:47.700
I know you weren't living together.

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:50.850
But our lawyer is very good.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:55.150
What do you want with me?

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.320
Want with you?

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.700
You make it sound like I'm trying 
to do something to you.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:05.510
If you help us, we can 
return the favor generously.

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:09.410
How about a job at a 
foreign securities company?

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:14.840
Is that how you broke up 
Richard and his girlfriend?

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:18.760
Goodnight, Nakata-kun.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.050
Come on, Nakata-kun!

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:25.150
Smile, smile!

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:27.180
<i>Once I exited customs,</i>

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:31.060
<i>Jeffrey dragged me around 
to various tourist spots,</i>

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.170
<i>uploading the photos to social media.</i>

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:38.860
Smile like you're looking at 
your lover in the distance.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:44.370
We'll have to retake that one.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:50.330
Am I bait for you to catch Richard?

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:53.330
It's important to properly manage your 
true intentions and stated reasons.

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:58.300
You actually like Richard, don't you?

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:03.680
What makes you say that?

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:08.520
That gemstone is an alexandrite.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.810
Henry-san was wearing it
 in the photo with the dog.

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:14.920
It appears green in natural light,

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:18.820
but it looked red on the plane,
so I didn't notice.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:24.410
You had to choose between Richard 
and your brother, too. Just like me.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:25.660
Shut up!

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:28.740
I'm sorry. I...

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:33.200
When the will was unsealed,

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:36.400
Henry came down with a psychogenic disease.

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:40.510
The glances of our relatives all turned cold.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:43.840
If not for me, he'd be alone.

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:51.850
After Richard disappeared, my brother 
gave me his treasured gemstone.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:57.340
I only asked Richard's girlfriend 
to break up with him

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.480
because I didn't want Henry to die.

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:09.860
But you're free to judge 
me however you want.

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:23.270
My head hurts. Am I coming 
down with something?

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.080
Hello?

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.320
Tokyo      Ginza

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.320
<b>Shiseido Parlour</b>

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:40.320
This is...

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:46.990
<b>TSURUYA</b>

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:46.990
Tsuruya Ginza

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.280
<b>Ginza Mizukashi</b>

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:56.210
<i>Did you cross the sea, too?</i>

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:00.500
<i>You can find me among your friends.</i>

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:04.470
<i>For more details, examine your pamphlet.</i>

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.480
<i>Where are you, Richard?</i>
